diff --git a/patches/README.md b/patches/README.md
index 1fbf64d..249208a 100644
--- a/patches/README.md
+++ b/patches/README.md
@@ -1,5 +1,26 @@
 # OpenDrop patches for iOS 26
 
+## owl-tx-backpressure.patch
+
+Applied to the OWL source by `install.sh` before building (it is not part of
+the OpenDrop patch series below). On the MT7922 monitor interface, a full
+nonblocking transmit queue returned `EAGAIN` and OWL discarded hundreds of
+outgoing TCP ACK frames. A large incoming upload then stopped despite a strong
+radio signal. Keep unicast and multicast frames queued and retry after 2 ms
+when injection reports backpressure; do not mutate a frame while attempting a
+send, since it must be safe to retry. The patch is idempotently checked against
+the OWL checkout so a later install does not double-apply it.
+
+## owl-ios26-action-trailer.patch
+
+Applied to OWL by `install.sh` before building. Current iPhone AWDL MIF and
+PSF frames carry two bytes after their last complete TLV. OWL used to reject
+every such frame before marking a peer's MIF as received, so the peer never
+became valid even while its channel sequence and data frames were visible.
+Accept exactly the observed two-byte trailer; retain rejection of all other
+incomplete TLV tails. Local regression tests cover the accepted and rejected
+cases.
+
 These patch OpenDrop 0.13.0 in place, inside whatever virtualenv you installed
 it into. That means they do not survive rebuilding the venv, so they live here
 and get reapplied:
@@ -19,7 +40,7 @@ with every earlier patch already applied, so they only apply in this order:
 ios26-airdrop recv-window py314-send mdns-repeat find-report tls-keylog
 upload-arms ask-confirm mdns-reannounce threaded-server url-items
 zeroconf-update-service salvage-truncated salvage-trim send-multifile
-send-status send-stall
+send-status send-stall incomplete-response verified-archive
 ```
 
 The install loop in the [main README](../README.md) uses exactly that order.
@@ -502,3 +523,22 @@ without tripping it.
 Only the raw-framed path is covered, since it is the one the default arm uses.
 The response read after a complete upload stays unbounded: if the phone dies
 after receiving every byte, that wait is still TCP's to end.
+
+## opendrop-incomplete-response.patch
+
+An interrupted upload used to return HTTP 200 and log "File(s) received" even
+when only half of the declared bytes arrived. Compare the received body with
+`TotalBytes`, retain the raw archive if any file or byte is missing, and return
+HTTP 500 with an explicit incomplete log entry. This reports failure honestly;
+it does not repair an AWDL link that stops delivering data.
+
+
+## opendrop-verified-archive.patch
+
+On iOS 26 `TotalBytes` can describe the uncompressed archive, while the
+`dvzip` bytes on the wire are smaller. Treat a transfer as complete only
+when the decoded cpio has its parsed `TRAILER!!!` and all extracted file
+members arrived whole. For gzip uploads, also require the gzip end marker.
+A missing trailer or incomplete member retains the raw archive and returns
+HTTP 500. A complete compressed archive returns HTTP 200 even when its wire
+size differs from `TotalBytes`.
